# Lambda runtimes - always the current runtime, never an old one

An old runtime eventually stops getting security patches from AWS, and anything still running on
it carries known CVEs nobody will fix. AWS also blocks creating, then updating, functions on a
retired runtime, so an old pin becomes a deploy that suddenly fails.

## The rule

- **Node.js Lambdas use `nodejs24.x` or newer.** Never `nodejs22.x`, `nodejs20.x`, `nodejs18.x`
  or anything older. When AWS releases a newer Node.js runtime, prefer the newest one.
- **Container-image Lambdas** use the matching AWS base image, for example
  `public.ecr.aws/lambda/nodejs:24` or newer - never an older tag, and never `latest`.
- **Other languages** use the newest runtime AWS currently supports for that language - never one
  AWS has announced a deprecation date for.
- **Write the runtime once** (template `Globals`, a shared constant, one deploy-script variable)
  so the next upgrade is a one-line change.
- **Match local tooling**: `engines.node` in `package.json` (and `.nvmrc`) on the same major.

## Existing functions on an old runtime

If your change edits that function's configuration or deploy path, upgrade the runtime in the
same change and say so. If that is out of scope or risky (native modules, old SDK v2 imports),
flag it to the user explicitly - never copy the old runtime into new code.

## Dependencies

Most CVEs arrive through packaged libraries and layers, not the runtime. Prefer current versions
and run `npm audit` (or the language equivalent) before finishing.
